IPv4 Network Dataplane Router

A low-level IPv4 router dataplane implemented in C. The project handles packet forwarding across a virtual network topology by directly processing Ethernet, IPv4, ARP, and ICMP headers.

 Key Features

IPv4 Packet Forwarding: Implements Longest Prefix Match (LPM) using a binary trie to select the appropriate route and next-hop interface.
Dynamic ARP Resolution: Resolves IP addresses to MAC addresses, maintains an ARP cache, and queues packets while waiting for ARP replies.
ICMP Messages: Handles Echo Reply (Type 0), Destination Unreachable (Type 3), and Time Exceeded (Type 11).
Packet Validation: Verifies and recalculates IPv4 and ICMP checksums to detect corrupted packets.
TTL Management: Decrements the IPv4 TTL and discards expired packets, returning the appropriate ICMP error message.

 Architecture & Forwarding Logic

When a packet arrives, the router processes it through the following steps:
Header Processing: Extracts Ethernet, IPv4, or ARP headers and handles network-to-host byte order conversion.
ARP Processing: Responds to ARP requests addressed to the router, updates the ARP cache upon receiving replies, and forwards queued packets.
IPv4 Validation: Verifies the IP header checksum and discards invalid packets.
Local Delivery: Responds to ICMP Echo Requests addressed to the router's interfaces.
TTL Check: Decrements TTL and sends an ICMP Time Exceeded message if the packet expires.
Route Lookup: Uses LPM to find the best matching route. If no route exists, an ICMP Destination Unreachable message is generated.
Packet Forwarding: Resolves the next-hop MAC address, updates the Ethernet header, and forwards the packet. If the MAC address is unknown, the packet is queued until ARP resolution completes.

 Testing & Results

1. Packet Forwarding

Tested connectivity between hosts in different networks using ping h2 from h1 and ping h3 from h0. Successful replies and the absence of packet loss confirmed correct, bidirectional forwarding between the hosts.

2. Longest Prefix Match (LPM)

Implemented LPM using a binary trie. Routing prefixes are inserted bit by bit, and route lookup follows the destination IP address bits while retaining the last valid matching route.
Time complexity: (O(32)) for IPv4 addresses, since each address contains 32 bits.
This avoids scanning the entire routing table for every incoming packet.

3. ARP Resolution

Tested dynamic MAC address resolution using:

ip neigh flush all
arping 192.168.0.1

The tests generated ARP Requests (Who has 192.168.0.1?) and ARP Replies (192.168.0.1 is at ...), verified in Wireshark. The router responds to requests for its own addresses, caches learned mappings, and forwards queued packets after receiving the required ARP reply.

4. ICMP Error Handling

Tested TTL expiration using ping -t 1 h2 from h1. When the TTL expires, the router drops the packet and sends an ICMP Time Exceeded message (Type 11, Code 0). The error message was observed in the terminal and the corresponding ICMP packets were verified in Wireshark.

 Tech Stack

Language: C
Concepts: IPv4 Routing, Ethernet, ARP, ICMP, Binary Tries, Packet Manipulation, Checksums, Queue Data Structures, Byte Order.

 Build and Run

Prerequisites: Linux, gcc, and make.
Compile the router from the project root:
make
